  "text": "Let's evaluate the product step by step.\n\nFirst, we will use the change of base formula for logarithms:\n\n\\[\n\\log_a(b) = \\frac{\\log_c(b)}{\\log_c(a)}\n\\]\n\nUsing this formula, we can rewrite each term in the product:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4})} = \\frac{\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log(5)}}{\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-4})}{\\log(5)}} = \\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log(5^{k^2-4})}\n\\]\n\nUsing the power rule of logarithms, we can simplify this further:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log(5^{k^2-4})} = \\frac{(k^2-1) \\log(5)}{(k^2-4) \\log(5)} = \\frac{k^2-1}{k^2-4}\n\\]\n\nNow we can rewrite the entire product as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k^2-1}{k^2-4} \\cdot \\frac{k^2-4}{k^2-9} \\cdot \\frac{k^2-9}{k^2-16} \\cdots \\frac{k^2-3968}{k^2-3969}\n\\]\n\nNotice that this is a telescoping product, where most terms cancel out. The only terms that remain are the first numerator and the last denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k^2-1}{k^2-3969}\n\\]\n\nNow we need to evaluate this expression at $k=4, 5, 6, \\ldots, 63$:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4^2-1}{4^2-3969} \\cdot \\frac{5^2-1}{5^2-3969} \\cdot \\frac{6^2-1}{6^2-3969} \\cdots \\frac{63^2-1}{63^2-3969}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{3969} \\cdot \\frac{24}{3969} \\cdot \\frac{35}{3969} \\cdots \\frac{3968}{3969}\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out the 3969 from each term:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{15 \\cdot 24 \\cdot 35 \\cdots 3968}{3969 \\cdot 3969 \\cdot 3969 \\cdots 3969}\n\\]\n\nThe denominator is the product of 60 consecutive terms of 3969, which can be written as:\n\n\\[\n3969^{60}\n\\]\n\nThe numerator is the product of 60 terms of the form $5n-1$ for $n=3, 4, 5, \\ldots, 63$. We can write this as:\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{n=3}^{63} (5n-1)\n\\]\n\nNow we need to find the prime factorization of the numerator and the denominator. The prime factorization of 3969 is $3^4 \\cdot 139$. The prime factorization of the numerator is the product of the prime factorization of 5n-1 for $n=3, 4, 5, \\ldots, 63$.\n\nWe can use a calculator or a computer program to find the prime factorization of the numerator. The prime factorization of the numerator is:\n\n\\[\n3^4 \\cdot 139 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 11 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 23 \\cdot 29 \\cdot 31 \\cdot 37 \\cdot 41 \\cdot 43 \\cdot 47 \\cdot 53 \\cdot 59 \\cdot 61 \\cdot 67 \\cdot 71 \\cdot 73 \\cdot 79 \\cdot 83 \\cdot 89 \\cdot 97 \\cdot 101 \\cdot 103 \\cdot 107 \\cdot 109 \\cdot 113 \\cdot 127 \\cdot 131 \\cdot 137 \\cdot 139 \\cdot 149 \\cdot 151 \\cdot 157 \\cdot 163 \\cdot 167 \\cdot 173 \\cdot 179 \\cdot 181 \\cdot 191 \\cdot 193 \\cdot 197 \\cdot 199 \\cdot 211 \\cdot 223 \\cdot 227 \\cdot 229 \\cdot 233 \\cdot 239 \\cdot 241 \\cdot 251 \\cdot 257 \\cdot 263 \\cdot 269 \\cdot 271 \\cdot 277 \\cdot 281 \\cdot 283 \\cdot 293 \\cdot 307 \\cdot 311 \\cdot 313 \\cdot 317 \\cdot 331 \\cdot 337 \\cdot 347 \\cdot 349 \\cdot 353 \\cdot 359 \\cdot 367 \\cdot 373 \\cdot 379 \\cdot 383 \\cdot 389 \\cdot 397\n\\]\n\nThe prime factorization of the denominator is:\n\n\\[\n3^4 \\cdot 139^{60}\n\\]\n\nNow we can find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of the numerator and the denominator. The gcd is:\n\n\\[\n3^4 \\cdot 139\n\\]\n\nThe fraction $\\frac{m}{n}$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3^4 \\cdot 139}{3^4 \\cdot 139^{60}} = \\frac{1}{139^{59}}\n\\]\n\nThe values of $m$ and $n$ are 1 and 139^59, respectively. The sum $m+n$ is:\n\n\\[\n1 + 139^{59}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1 + 139^{59}}\n\\]",
